/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Reflectance ρ(λ) is the fraction of light arriving at a surface that the surface sends back at wavelength λ.
   - Why a curve and not one number: every material absorbs some wavelengths and scatters others.
   - Chlorophyll absorbs blue light near 0.45 µm and red light near 0.67 µm for photosynthesis.
   - From about 0.75 to 1.3 µm, on the near-infrared plateau, a healthy leaf is bright.
   - Stressed or sparse vegetation has lower NIR and higher red, so the red edge weakens.
   - Soil has a smooth curve that rises slowly from visible to shortwave infrared: 10% at 0.45 µm, 18% at 0.66 µm and 33% at 2.2 µm in the figure for dry soil.
   - Clear water is brightest in blue (8–9% at 0.40–0.45 µm in the figure), falls through green and red, and is almost black in NIR (2% at 0.83 µm, 1% at 1.1 µm).
   - ρNIR is the near-infrared reflectance and ρred is the red reflectance; both are unitless fractions from 0 to 1.
   - High NIR and low red give values near +1 for green vegetation.
*/
