/* book Worked example 2: Q: A land satellite flies a circular sun-synchronous orbit at h = 705 km. (a) Find its orbital period and the number of orbits per day. (b) Find how far apart, at the equator, two successive ground tracks fall. (c) With a 185 km swath, why can it not image every point each day? (d) Check that a satellite at 35 786 km is geostationary. Take R = 6378 km and μ = 398 600 km³/s².
   1. (a) Orbit radius a = 6378 + 705 = 7083 km. a³ = 7083³ = 3.553 × 10¹¹ km³.
   2. a³ ÷ μ = 3.553 × 10¹¹ ÷ 398 600 = 8.915 × 10⁵ s²; √ = 944.2 s; T = 2π × 944.2 = 5933 s = 98.9 min.
   3. Orbits per day = 86 400 ÷ 5933 = 14.56.
   4. (b) The earth turns under the orbit, so each new track falls west of the last by the equator length ÷ orbits per day: 40 075 ÷ 14.56 = 2752 km.
   5. (c) One day's 14.56 swaths of 185 km cover 14.56 × 185 = 2694 km of the 40 075 km equator, about 7 percent. The orbit is chosen so the tracks shift a little each day and fill the gaps; for Landsat 8 at this height the pattern repeats every 16 days.
   6. (d) a = 6378 + 35 786 = 42 164 km; T = 2π √(42 164³ ÷ 398 600) = 86 164 s = 23 h 56 min, one sidereal day, so the satellite keeps pace with the turning earth.
   Answer: The period is 5933 s (98.9 min), 14.56 orbits a day, with tracks 2752 km apart at the equator, so a 185 km swath needs many days (16 for Landsat 8) to cover the globe. At 35 786 km the period is 86 164 s, one sidereal day. */
