/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An image is a grid of picture elements, pixels.
   - GSD is the ground sampling distance in metres; H is the sensor height above ground in metres; θ is the instantaneous field of view (IFOV) of one detector element, in radians.
   - A sensor at H = 700 km with θ = 10 μrad has GSD = 700 000 m × 10 × 10⁻⁶ = 7 m.
   - When one pixel holds two land covers, such as part field and part road, it is a mixed pixel.
   - Spectral resolution describes the number and width of wavelength bands.
   - Radiometric resolution is the number of discrete levels used to record radiance.
   - ΔL is the quantisation step in radiance units; Lmax and Lmin are the radiance limits of the sensor; n is the number of bits per pixel. 2ⁿ levels have 2ⁿ − 1 steps between them (256 levels, DN 0 to 255, have 255 steps), which is why the rang
   - A smaller ΔL means higher radiometric resolution.
   - Temporal resolution is the revisit time, also called the repeat cycle: the time between two images of the same ground point.
   - The four resolutions compete.
*/
