/* book exam traps: Finer spatial resolution means a smaller ground pixel size. 5 m is finer than 30 m, not coarser. | Higher radiometric resolution means more bits and more levels, not a bigger pixel or more bands. | Spectral resolution is about band number and width, not about how small a pixel is. | A mixed pixel is not a broken measurement; it is the average of covers inside the pixel. | Temporal resolution is revisit time, not the time taken to process an image. And a radiance difference smaller than one quantisation step can vanish: two fields 0.20 apart both read DN 128 on an 8-bit sensor with a 0.471 step. */
